#  Azure DevOps: Introduction & Overview

## 1. What is Azure DevOps?

**Azure DevOps** is a platform developed by Microsoft that provides a **collection of services** for DevOps engineers to improve and automate the software development life cycle (SDLC).

- It is not a single tool — it is a **suite of integrated services**, each targeting a specific phase of the SDLC.
- The goal is to reduce the time and effort required to ship new features, bug fixes, and application versions to customers.
- All services are available under one platform, eliminating the need to integrate and maintain multiple separate tools.

---

## 2. The Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC)

Every software project — regardless of organisation or technology — goes through a set of standard phases before a feature or fix reaches end users.

```
1. Planning      → Define requirements, scope, timelines
2. Design        → HLD, LLD, architecture decisions
3. Development   → Write the actual code / feature
4. Testing       → Validate correctness, performance, security
5. Deployment    → Release the application to users
6. Monitoring    → Track health, performance, errors in production
```

**Without automation:** Each of these phases is performed manually. Developers write code, testers manually run tests, operations teams manually deploy — resulting in slow release cycles (e.g., 3 months per feature).

**With DevOps + automation:** Manual bottlenecks are eliminated, continuous integration and deployment pipelines are in place, and the same release cycle can be reduced to weeks or even days.

---

## 3. How DevOps Improves the SDLC

DevOps engineers participate across **all phases** of the SDLC and introduce automation wherever manual effort exists.

| SDLC Phase | Manual pain point | DevOps automation |
|---|---|---|
| Planning | Scattered requirements, no tracking | Agile boards (Jira / Azure Boards) |
| Development | Code changes not tracked or versioned | Version Control System (Git / Azure Repos) |
| Testing | Tests run manually after every change | Automated test suites triggered by CI pipelines |
| Deployment | Manual, error-prone server deployments | CD pipelines with automated rollout |
| Infrastructure | Manually provisioned servers | IaC with Terraform / ARM Templates |
| Monitoring | Reactive, manual log checks | Automated alerting and dashboards |

---

## 4. Azure DevOps — The Platform

**URL:** [https://dev.azure.com](https://dev.azure.com)

### Getting started

1. Go to [dev.azure.com](https://dev.azure.com).
2. Click **Start free with GitHub** (recommended — keeps Azure and Azure DevOps under the same account).
3. Create an **Organisation** and then a **Project**.

### Project settings

| Setting | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| **Visibility** | Private (for organisation projects) |
| **Version control** | Git |
| **Work item process** | Agile or Scrum |

Once the project is created, the left sidebar shows all five Azure DevOps services.

---

## 5. Azure DevOps Services

### Overview

| Service | Icon | SDLC Phase | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Boards** | 📋 | Planning & Design | Work item tracking, sprint planning, backlog management |
| **Repos** | 📁 | Development | Git-based version control for source code |
| **Pipelines** | ⚙️ | Testing & Deployment | CI/CD automation — build, test, and deploy |
| **Test Plans** | 🧪 | Testing | Test case management, manual and automated testing |
| **Artifacts** | 📦 | Post-deployment | Store build outputs — JAR, WAR, Docker images, npm packages |

---

### Boards

- Equivalent to **Jira** in functionality.
- Used by product owners, scrum masters, developers, and QA engineers for **planning and tracking work**.
- Supports **Epics → Features → User Stories → Tasks** hierarchy.
- Work items can be organised into **Sprints** and **Backlogs**.
- Tracks progress visually through Kanban boards and sprint burn-down charts.

**Who uses it:** Product Owner, Business Analyst, Development Team, QA Team, Managers.

---

### Repos

- A **Git-based version control system** hosted on Azure DevOps.
- Developers push source code here; DevOps engineers store Terraform scripts, Kubernetes manifests, Ansible playbooks, and pipeline definitions.
- Supports **branching strategies** (feature branches, GitFlow, trunk-based development).
- Also supports connecting to **external repositories** (GitHub, Bitbucket).

**Who uses it:** Developers, DevOps Engineers.

---

### Pipelines

- The **CI/CD engine** of Azure DevOps.
- Automatically triggered when code is pushed to Repos (or GitHub).
- A pipeline consists of multiple **stages**:

```
Developer pushes code to Repos
        │
        ▼
[Pipeline triggers automatically]
        │
        ├── Stage 1: Build        → Compile code, run unit tests
        ├── Stage 2: Test         → Run automated integration/QA tests
        ├── Stage 3: Package      → Create artefact (JAR, Docker image)
        └── Stage 4: Deploy       → Push to staging / production environment
```

- Pipelines are defined as **YAML files** stored in the repository (pipeline-as-code).
- Supports deployment to Azure VMs, AKS, App Service, and more.

**Who uses it:** DevOps Engineers, Developers.

---

### Test Plans

- Used by **QA / Test Engineers** to manage the testing process.
- Provides tools for:
- Creating and organising **test cases** and **test suites**.
- Planning test coverage for each feature.
- Running **manual tests** directly from the browser.
- Linking automated tests from Pipelines to test plans for traceability.
- Replaces external tools like HP ALM or Microsoft Test Manager.

**Who uses it:** QA Engineers, Test Leads.

---

### Artifacts

- A **package and artefact repository** built into Azure DevOps.
- Stores the **outputs of CI/CD pipeline runs**:
- Java: `.jar` / `.war` files
- .NET: NuGet packages
- Node.js: npm packages
- Docker: container images (via Azure Container Registry integration)
- Can be used as a **private package feed** — teams publish internal libraries here and consume them in builds.
- Replaces external tools like JFrog Artifactory or Sonatype Nexus for basic use cases.

**Who uses it:** DevOps Engineers, Developers.

---

## 6. How Each Service Maps to the SDLC

```
SDLC Phase          Azure DevOps Service
──────────          ────────────────────
Planning        →   Boards (Epics, Stories, Sprints, Backlog)
Design          →   Boards (tasks, design work items)
Development     →   Repos (source code version control)
Testing         →   Pipelines (automated test stage) + Test Plans
Deployment      →   Pipelines (CD stage → Azure VM, AKS, App Service)
Post-release    →   Artifacts (store build outputs) + Monitoring (external)
```

---

## 7. Azure DevOps vs Traditional Toolchains

Many organisations achieve the same outcome using a combination of open-source and third-party tools. Azure DevOps consolidates these into a single platform.

| Capability | Traditional (open-source / third-party) | Azure DevOps |
|---|---|---|
| Planning | Jira | Azure Boards |
| Version control | GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket | Azure Repos |
| CI/CD | Jenkins, GitLab CI, GitHub Actions, Argo CD | Azure Pipelines |
| Test management | HP ALM, Zephyr | Azure Test Plans |
| Artefact storage | JFrog Artifactory, Nexus | Azure Artifacts |

**Advantage of Azure DevOps:** Everything is integrated in one place — no need to configure webhooks between Jira → Jenkins → Nexus → Argo CD and maintain each tool separately.

**When to still use the traditional stack:** When the organisation is multi-cloud or already has a mature Jenkins/GitHub Actions setup, or when open-source flexibility is a priority.

---

## 8. Getting Started

1. Visit [https://dev.azure.com](https://dev.azure.com) → Sign in with a Microsoft or GitHub account.
2. Create an **Organisation**.
3. Create a **Project** (e.g., `nike-sales`) → set to **Private**.
4. Explore the left sidebar:
- **Boards** → create a work item and a sprint.
- **Repos** → clone the default Git repo and push some code.
- **Pipelines** → create a starter pipeline YAML.
- **Test Plans** → create a test suite.
- **Artifacts** → create a feed.

> Follow-up sessions will cover each service in depth with hands-on CI/CD pipeline implementations and project-based examples.

---

## 9. Summary

| Concept | Key Takeaway |
|---|---|
| Azure DevOps | Microsoft's all-in-one platform for implementing DevOps practices across the SDLC |
| SDLC | Plan → Design → Develop → Test → Deploy → Monitor |
| DevOps goal | Automate manual stages to reduce release cycle time |
| Boards | Agile planning and work item tracking (equivalent to Jira) |
| Repos | Git-based source code and infrastructure-as-code storage |
| Pipelines | CI/CD automation — triggered on code push, runs build/test/deploy stages |
| Test Plans | Test case management and manual/automated test execution |
| Artifacts | Stores build outputs (JARs, images, packages) from pipeline runs |

